A||||10||william_thorniii||Shipping a Body Kit From Vancouver - Cincinnati?||||5||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||04-04-2001||02:58 PM||||Friends,<P>Does anyone out there have experience in shipping a body kit?  I have recently found an IMSA kit (front & rear quarter panels, front & rear fascia & side skirts... no front or rear spoiler) in Vancouver, BC Canada.  I now need to have it shipped to Cincinnati.  I estimated my costs of gas to be around $300 to drive out there and back in a pick up... so shipping is a much more viable route.  Any suggestions on who to check with or where?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-04-2001||03:56 PM||||I would think that with crating and packing - you ain't gonna beat $300.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||04-04-2001||04:04 PM||||True enoug phil.... but I am not paying for crating and packing.  That is being paid for by the seller.  Considering the opportunity cost of spending a few days to drive the 5008 mi on the round trip and also the lost wages from missing a few days of work I concluded that it would cost significantly less to have the kit professionally shipped.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-04-2001||04:56 PM||||That stuff shouldn't be heavy, just bulky. I would contact a regular moving company (Allied, or Mayflower, one of those) They can put those things on a truck already headed that way as an add-on. Should be relatively cheap. And they can box the stuff. I think Fed-Ex and those have size limitations.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever2||04-04-2001||07:24 PM||||Don't forget Airborne can, within seconds, find a truck going that way which is not full, and make all the arrangements within a matter of minutes.  I don't know the cost but it will be competitive.<P> ||||reg||6
Z||000005||85Josh||04-04-2001||08:18 PM||||Cheever2, do you have a link to Airborne? I don't need it now, but it could be handy.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||04-04-2001||08:55 PM||||Airborne.com is the only one I know of, but I'm not seeing the ground shipping service that I was referring to.  So for that, the best thing to do would be call 1-800-AIRBORN (247-2676) or email cac.web@airborne.com<P>I have no connection with them now, but I worked in their computer room for 4 years and think they are a good company.  <BR>  ||||reg||6
Z||000007||rogergarrison||04-04-2001||10:40 PM||||the weights not the prob, its the bulk that ll kill you. They have surcharge for extra large or odd shaped shipments, I guarrantee itll be way over $300. Why not find a friend that can go for you and pick it up for a little fee. Then at least you know it wont be lost or damaged in shipment.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||04-05-2001||11:05 AM||||taji & cheever,  <P>I had thought of both of those options, but had not tried either of them.  Thanks for the suggestions.<P>Roger,<P>I only know of a few folks that are over on that half of the country.  One is Terry Owens.  I know that Terry is heading out this way in the spring, but I am sure that when he already has to drive from CA to KY it will be a long enough drive without heading up north to Vancouver and I am not sure if he will be driving a Fiero or not.  Then Mike Valentine in Colorado would probably do it for me, but Colorado is a long way from Vancouver.  I will try to contact both of them though.  Thanks for the suggestions.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>Oh... and Roger... this is for the car that I want you to paint in the future (If it ever gets done).<P>||||reg||1
